## Authentication

The provider authenticates as a **tenant OAuth client** using the OAuth 2.0
`client_credentials` grant. Create a client in the Relyance application (an admin can
generate one from the OAuth credentials screen) and pick a role:

- **Integration Configuration Management** — full connection management; use this for
  `terraform apply`.
- **Integration Configuration Viewer** — read-only; suited to audit tooling and plan-only
  CI credentials.

Two client authentication methods are supported (configure exactly one):

- `client_secret` — the client secret issued alongside the client id.
- `jwk_json` — a private JWK for `private_key_jwt` client assertions (RFC 7523), for
  organizations that prefer asymmetric keys over shared secrets.

{{ tffile "../../examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

All provider arguments can also come from the environment, which keeps credentials out of
configuration entirely:

| Argument        | Environment variable       |
|-----------------|----------------------------|
| `endpoint`      | `RELYANCE_ENDPOINT`        |
| `client_id`     | `RELYANCE_CLIENT_ID`       |
| `client_secret` | `RELYANCE_CLIENT_SECRET`   |
| `jwk_json`      | `RELYANCE_JWK_JSON`        |
| `audience`      | `RELYANCE_AUDIENCE`        |

## How secrets are handled

Non-secret auth fields go in `auth.params` (stored in state, fully diffable). Secret fields
go in `auth.secrets_wo`, which is **write-only**: values are sent to Relyance and persisted
in its secret store, and are never written to Terraform state or plan files. Because
write-only values cannot be diffed, rotating a secret is explicit — change the value **and
bump `auth.secrets_wo_version`** to have Terraform re-send it. The server-computed
`auth.credentials_fingerprint` attribute changes whenever stored credentials change, giving
you drift visibility without exposing material.

## What's available

- [`relyance_integration_connection` (resource)](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/Relyance/relyance/latest/docs/resources/integration_connection)
  — a vendor connection: naming, auth, scan capabilities, business-node mapping, lifecycle,
  and import (`<vendor>/<connection_id>`).
- [`relyance_integration_vendor` (data source)](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/Relyance/relyance/latest/docs/data-sources/integration_vendor)
  — a vendor's auth methods and field schemas, including which fields are secret.
- [`relyance_integration_connection` (data source)](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/Relyance/relyance/latest/docs/data-sources/integration_connection)
  — read a connection Terraform doesn't own (e.g. a browser-authorized OAuth vendor) and
  gate on its live status.
- [`relyance_business_nodes` (data source)](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/Relyance/relyance/latest/docs/data-sources/business_nodes)
  — look up business nodes by name instead of record id.

